  "authority_move": {
    "mechanic": "bloodsong.expose_weakness",
    "was": "SPLIT, with the campaign's one frozen DivergenceReceipt",
    "now": "COUPLED_AUTHORITATIVE_WITH_PAIR_PREVIEW; DIVERGENCES is empty",
    "position": "third of Phase 4 S7's seven authority moves",
    "why": "Expose Weakness amplifies every source's damage inside a live window on one enemy, which is a roster input, so the walk is the smallest engine that can see every input the rule reads. The pair engine's coarse row — one amp of the holder's whole running total, once for the fight, less the arming chain — is the honest one-attacker figure and survives as a declared THEORETICAL preview.",
    "plan_divergence_recorded": {
      "what": "The umbrella's authority table rules Bloodsong COUPLED_AUTHORITATIVE; this lands COUPLED_AUTHORITATIVE_WITH_PAIR_PREVIEW.",
      "why": "Plain COUPLED_AUTHORITATIVE is unconstructible for this mechanic against the tree's own machinery: item_support_effects._check_cross_participant_authority resolves a damage_modifier packet's authority through trigger_stream.CROSS_PARTICIPANT_AUTHORITIES, whose three members are the ones that say a second engine can see the mechanic. Bloodsong's pair pricer (damage._add_expose_weakness) still exists — golden pins it and deleting it is a pair-engine change this slice does not make — so a second engine *does* see it, and declaring otherwise would make the packet fail to build. The ruling's binding obligation ('pair and walk are not numerically equivalent; never unify inside a slice labelled a pure refactor') is met: the two readings are not unified, one is named authoritative and the other is tagged.",
      "flagged_for": "the slice's verify pass and the umbrella's owner; no plan document was edited by this lane."
    }
  },
